%[text] # Task 1: Explore the Cochlear Implant Model
%[text] **TIP:** **This script plays several audio clips. Run it one section at a time, and set the volume to a safe, comfortable level before playing the audio.**
%[text] 
%[text] This live script walks through Task 1 of Exercise 2 entirely from the command line, so you can follow along without clicking through the Simulink canvas. You will **hear** the model (original vs. reconstructed speech), **see** what it does (the filter bank and the per-channel electrical pulses), and **tweak** it (add noise, switch filters etc.).
%%
%[text] ## Background: How hearing works, and how an implant restores it
%[text] In normal hearing, sound reaches the **cochlea**, where different regions respond to different frequencies. High frequencies stimulate the base and low frequencies the apex, effectively separating sound by frequency. Hair cells then convert this response into signals sent to the brain. When these hair cells are damaged, a **cochlear implant bypasses them and directly stimulates the auditory nerve**. A signal processor separates the incoming sound into frequency bands, which are then converted into electrical pulses and delivered through electrodes positioned along the cochlea. **Our Simulink model captures that signal-processing path**, taking the input sound, separating it into **eight frequency channels**, and generating the electrical pulses used for stimulation.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:41a6)
%%
%[text] ## Setup: Load the model
model = "CochlearImplantModel";
load_system(model) %[output:0362bba7]
%%
%[text] ## 1. Hear it: Original and Reconstructed speech
%[text] Configure the model for the default case: 
%[text] - A clean speech signal
%[text] - FIR filter bank
%[text] - No denoising
%[text] - Channels played sequentially. \
setDefaultConfiguration(model)
%[text] Run the model. After the simulation completes, the input and reconstructed signals are available in the base workspace along with the sample rate `fs`.
sim(model);
%%
%[text] Listen to the **original** speech that enters the implant:
soundsc(OrigSignal, fs)
%%
%[text] Now listen to the **reconstructed speech**, synthesized from the eight channels of electrical pulses. **It sounds coarser and more robotic than the original**, but the speech is still understandable.
soundsc(SynSignal, fs)
%%
%[text] ## 2. See it: The filter bank and the electrical pulses
%[text] The filter bank separates the incoming speech into eight overlapping frequency bands. These bands are not evenly spaced. Because speech carries more information at lower frequencies, the lower-frequency bands are narrower, giving us **finer resolution where more of the speech information is concentrated**.
%[text] Now let’s look at the filter bank itself. **`plotFilterBankResponses`** plots the frequency response of the filter bank currently selected in the model. Here, we’re using the **cascaded, multirate, multistage FIR filter bank**, and we can see all eight channels overlaid, from Channel 0 at the lowest frequencies to Channel 7 at the highest.
plotFilterBankResponses %[output:55e473e0]
%%
%[text] To see the filter bank in action, switch the input to a **chirp**, a tone that sweeps smoothly from low to high frequency, and run the model again. 
%[text] **The filter bank itself does not change; the chirp simply moves through its frequency range, activating each band in turn.**
set_param(model + "/Input_Source", source_type="Chirp")
sim(model);
channels = logChannelActivation(model);
%%
%[text] Here we plot all **eight filter-bank outputs over time**, one for each channel. Channel 0 captures the lowest frequencies and Channel 7 the highest.
%[text] As the chirp sweeps from low to high frequency, the energy moves sequentially from Ch0 through Ch7, showing how the filter bank maps different frequencies to different channels..
plotChannelActivation(channels, fs) %[output:251a8d88]
%%
%[text] ## 3. Tweak it: Add noise and toggle filter choice
%[text] Noise is a significant challenge for cochlear implant users. The model lets you add noise at the input and apply an optional denoise stage so you can hear the effect of each.
%[text] Return to the speech signal and turn on the noise option to simulate a noisy listening environment, then listen to how much harder the reconstruction is to understand.
set_param(model + "/Input_Source", source_type="Speech signal", noise_type="on")
set_param(model + "/Denoise", denoise="off")
sim(model);
%%
%[text] Listen to the **noisy** **original** speech that enters the implant:
soundsc(OrigSignal, fs)
%%
%[text] Now listen to the **reconstructed speech:**
soundsc(SynSignal, fs)
%%
%[text] **Denoise:** Turn on the soft-threshold denoise stage:
set_param(model + "/Denoise", denoise="on")
sim(model);
%%
%[text] Now listen again to the **reconstructed speech.** Some noise is removed, but not all. There is no perfect solution, and any denoising must be weighed against its cost.
soundsc(SynSignal, fs)
%%
%[text] **Switch the filter bank:** 
%[text] The model offers two different ways to create the same eight frequency bands. The **parallel IIR design** runs all eight filters at the full input rate. The **multirate FIR design** progressively downsamples the signal, so the later stages operate at much lower rates, with the final stages running at just **one-eighth of the input rate**. 
%[text] That reduces the processing required and makes the FIR design well suited for the **low-power DSPs used in cochlear implant processors**. It also gives us **sharper, flatter frequency bands**.
%[text] Let’s compare the two by plotting the **IIR filter response**, and then listening to the reconstructed speech.
set_param(model + "/Filter bank signal processing", filter_type="Parallel single-rate SOS IIR")
plotFilterBankResponses %[output:26871608]
set_param(model + "/Input_Source", noise_type="off")
set_param(model + "/Denoise", denoise="off")
sim(model);
%[text] Now listen again to the **reconstructed speech:**
soundsc(SynSignal, fs)
%%
%[text] ## Reset and close
%[text] Restore the default configuration so the model is left clean for the next task.
setDefaultConfiguration(model)
close_system(model, 0)

